How often have you been asked how much money it will cost to handle 1,000,000 users (or any other amount of users) with your service? Eventually, the question becomes: how many resources will I need to handle a certain number of users with an acceptable service level? While we all live in the world of elastic resources and autoscaling, this is still quite a common question. Why? Well, in big companies, procurement departments don't really like "unexpected" expenses. Many companies plan their budgets for one year, so any significant deviations might be a problem. Also, in companies where the so-called platform team or any other team handling infrastructure needs to know what they can expect, they need to set up limits to safely run your service alongside many others. Additionally, knowing the capacity upfront allows you to enable cost-saving plans on most popular public cloud providers.

So how can we do this forecasting, or more properly, capacity planning, while being at the very beginning of our development or even at a conceptual level? The good point is that most probably you don't need to be extremely precise in your calculations; however, you have to be accurate. Capacity planning can generally be a very complex task, so let's define some boundaries.
Suppose we have a .NET service, let it be a stateless REST API service. Also suppose this service has some database. The total capacity will be the combined capacity of the service and the database. Let's focus on the service itself since the database will have a different model of scalability. Most probably you will use horizontal scaling for the services and vertical scaling with read-replicas for the database. I would like to narrow the scope, that I will do the capacity planning for stateless REST API. Similar principles might be applied for the database as well, however, let's leave that for the end of the article and focus on planning resources for the service only.

At the beginning we have an expected user count, but this is not enough. We need to translate these users into requests per second/minute and also into read/write split patterns. To be more precise, you also need to estimate the amount of sent/received bytes, average payload sizes, peak traffic patterns, request complexity, caching effectiveness, and session duration. But for our example, the first two parameters are enough. Why these two? First of all, each request consumes compute resources - which means it costs money. The read/write split is important because usually read and write operations have different complexity. For example, a read request might have just one database query via Dapper, while on the other hand, a write operation might involve a complex DDD approach with complex models and Entity Framework Core.

How to do the translation? It is a hard task, but you can approach it in several practical ways:
First, if you have an existing similar service, analyze its metrics. Look at your monitoring data and calculate the average requests per user per day/hour. Then factor in usage patterns – are there peak hours when most users are active?

If you're starting from scratch, make educated guesses based on user behavior modeling. For example, a typical mobile app user might perform 20-30 actions per day, with 80% concentrated during active hours. A B2B service might see steady usage during business hours with minimal weekend traffic.
You can also look at comparable services or industry benchmarks. Many cloud providers publish whitepapers with average workload patterns for different application types. While not exact, these give you a reasonable starting point. For read/write patterns, think about your application's nature. Social media platforms might have a 95/5 read/write split, while data entry applications could be closer to 50/50. CMS systems might be 99/1 for public-facing content.

